Why ShareAlike, and what it does not mean
-----------------------------------------
ShareAlike is here on purpose: the point of this project is that the methodology
stays open. A closed-source derivative that takes the rules and gives nothing
back is the outcome ShareAlike prevents.

It does **not** mean any of the following:

```
· Internal use: copying the rules into a company wiki, unpublished, triggers no
  obligation beyond attribution.
· Quoting: citing a paragraph, a rule, or a table in a paper or an article is
  quoting, not adapting — attribution is enough.
· Collections: bundling the document unmodified alongside other material is a
  "collection", and ShareAlike does not spread to the rest of the bundle.
· Code: ShareAlike follows the written content only. Software that *implements*
  these rules is not required to be CC BY-SA; it is the software's own license.
· GPLv3: CC BY-SA 4.0 is one-way compatible with GPLv3, so an adaptation may
  also be distributed under GPLv3 if that is what a project needs.
```

How to attribute
----------------
A form like this is sufficient:

  Based on QuantCordis (https://github.com/QuantCordis/QuantCordis),
  licensed under CC BY-SA 4.0. Changes were made.

Why split the licenses
----------------------
The rule list and research notes are the part of this project meant to be read,
quoted, and reused in other people's methodology. They carry a content license
that keeps adaptations open and asks for attribution.

The code is meant to be run and shipped inside other software. It carries MIT,
with the warranty disclaimer software needs. Creative Commons themselves do not
recommend their licenses for software, and MIT does not express attribution well
for prose — hence the split.

Code snippets inside documents
------------------------------
Short code samples that appear *inside* a .md file may be copied and used under
the MIT License, so that reading a document never puts a license obstacle in the
way of using the code it explains.

